{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>A white se'et or etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim they stated the reason for His saying ״לְבָנָה״ (white): to say that it is not reddish, whereas a baheret that is white is reddish. And from where do we know to assign reddishness to the se'et and smoothness to a white baheret? Scripture says (Leviticus 13:20) ״נֶגַע צָרַעַת״ — meaning, at the end of the matter. And it appears that the Tanna did not need the inclusion of ״נֶגַע צָרַעַת״ except for the thinness (pitude) of the se'et: lest you say, since its whiteness is not intense, if thinness comes upon it the whiteness would darken — Scripture comes to teach [otherwise]. But not for the smoothness of the baheret, for even had there been no inclusion of ״נֶגַע צָרַעַת״ I would not declare pure a smooth baheret, for we do not find that there is impurity in a se'et more than in a baheret." "And the meaning of the verse is thus: ״שְׂאֵת לְבָנָה אוֹ בַהֶרֶת לְבָנָה״ — even though it is reddish, for the appearance of the baheret has in it se'et and it is not declared pure on account of the great manifestation of tzara'at in it, and the inclusion of ״נֶגַע צָרַעַת״ was needed only for the thinness of the se'et. And even though the Tanna stated two distinctions, he said them in passing (agav amarah). And know that it is not specifically se'et, but the same applies to the sapachat of the baheret and that of the se'et — they impart impurity whether smooth or thin. So is proven in tractate Negaim (chapter 1, mishnah 2), and its reason is from the fact that ״נֶגַע צָרַעַת״ included all — all four appearances are equal in this law."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "Read פָּתוּךְ (pituach, 'thinness') per rabbinic dermatological terminology (opposed to חֲלָקָה, smoothness); the word is consistently rendered 'thinness' throughout these two comments."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:58.208782619Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}